<ansi fg="black-bold">.:</ansi> <ansi fg="magenta">Help for </ansi><ansi fg="command">alignment</ansi>

A player or mobs <ansi fg="command">alignment</ansi> reflects their reputation and behavior.

You can see your alignment at the top of your <ansi fg="command">stats</ansi> information.

The possible alignments are:

    <ansi fg="yellow"> 80 to  100:</ansi> <ansi fg="holy">Holy</ansi>
    <ansi fg="yellow"> 60 to   79:</ansi> <ansi fg="good">Good</ansi>
    <ansi fg="yellow"> 40 to   59:</ansi> <ansi fg="virtuous">Virtuous</ansi>
    <ansi fg="yellow"> 20 to   39:</ansi> <ansi fg="lawful">Lawful</ansi>
    <ansi fg="yellow">-19 to   19:</ansi> <ansi fg="neutral">Neutral</ansi>
    <ansi fg="yellow">-20 to  -39:</ansi> <ansi fg="misguided">Misguided</ansi>
    <ansi fg="yellow">-40 to  -59:</ansi> <ansi fg="corrupt">Corrupt</ansi>
    <ansi fg="yellow">-60 to  -79:</ansi> <ansi fg="evil">Evil</ansi>
    <ansi fg="yellow">-80 to -100:</ansi> <ansi fg="unholy">Unholy</ansi>

Alignment naturally decays over time, eventually settling back at <ansi fg="neutral">Neutral</ansi>.
Mobs you encounter may behave differently towards you depending upon their 
alignment, and yours. Some mobs with an alignment of <ansi fg="virtuous">Virtuous</ansi> or higher
may see unscrupulous acts and intervene.

When PVP is enabled, players may freely attack others with an <ansi fg="evil">Evil</ansi> alignment
or lower, and receive no penalty.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Your company's alignment</ansi>

Each companion has their own alignment, shown the same way (-100 most
evil, 100 most good) by <ansi fg="command">company alignment</ansi> and
<ansi fg="command">company status</ansi> (<ansi fg="command">help company</ansi>).
Left to themselves, companions drift toward the rest of the company: every
few minutes, each one moves a little way toward the average alignment of
everyone else in it (you included, if you're online). The whole company
pulls together over time.

A companion far from that average grows uneasy and loses loyalty; kept
close, they gain it back. Loyalty runs 0 to 100. Losing too much of it
too long costs you the companion outright: at 0 loyalty they lose faith
and desert, taking nothing with them.

This also gates who will join you. A candidate whose alignment is too far
from your company's average won't take service with you at all;
<ansi fg="command">company inspect</ansi> tells you before you pay whether they
would join, and whether they'd join uneasy.

Class promotion checks it too: a good route at +30 or higher, an evil route
at -30 or lower, at levels 10 and 30 (<ansi fg="command">help elite</ansi>).

See also: <ansi fg="command">help company</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help standing</ansi>.

Sparing a surrendered foe raises your alignment by 5; execution lowers it by 5 instead of ordinary kill alignment. Company alignment remains its living members’ average. Companions react with loyalty by their own beliefs (help mercy).
